-// GORILLA UNLEASHED - SATELLITE MODE: slow or satellite link levels.
-//
-// One pref, gorilla.linkmode: 0 = off (the shipped values), 1 = "Satellite" (fast but far: Starlink, GEO
-// broadband), 2 = "Very slow link" (1-60 KB/s). The level's values are written to the DEFAULT branch, so a value
-// the user set by hand always wins, and going back to 0 restores the shipped defaults exactly. Nothing here makes
-// a connection: every value makes the browser fetch less, wait longer, keep what it has, or ask sites for lighter
-// pages (D-157-00, D-157-30, D-157-33). Values: docs/NETWORK-AND-SATELLITE-STUDY.md sections 7.2 and 7.3.
-
-const LINKMODE_PREF = "gorilla.linkmode";
-// Mobile pages (D-157-33, maintainer 2026-10-04: "combine the best of the worlds", a 13-inch tablet that asks for
-// the mobile site gets it and is extremely fast). While a level is on and this is true, sites are told the browser
-// is Firefox for Android (Mozilla's own Android values, the ones resistFingerprinting reports there), so they send
-// their phone pages. One site can be sent back to its desktop version from the toolbar button (DESKTOP_SITE_PERM).
-const MOBILE_PREF = "gorilla.linkmode.mobile_pages";
-const DESKTOP_SITE_PERM = "gorilla-desktop-site";
-// No JavaScript in Very slow link (D-157-33; real sites measured 2026-10-04 over the owner's link with pictures and
-// fonts already off: BBC News 1,175 KB -> 101 KB, CNN 11,831 KB -> 607 KB, Reuters 2,142 KB -> 315 KB, the text
-// readable on all six sites tried). Per tab (BrowsingContext.allowJavascript), decided before the page is requested;
-// a site that needs scripts (webmail, maps) is allowed from the toolbar button (JS_SITE_PERM). javascript.enabled
-// itself is never touched, so the browser's own pages keep working.
-const NO_JS_PREF = "gorilla.linkmode.no_javascript";
-const JS_SITE_PERM = "gorilla-javascript-site";
